// One bit of a summary. A summary holds two masks of fifteen bits: bit i says
// parameter i may be kept, bit 15 + i says the callee may break the ownership
// of the elements of parameter i (see esc_dbit). Parameters past the
// fifteenth have no bit, so they are reported as kept.
esc_bit(int m, int i) {
  int c = 1;
  if(i < 15) {
    c = (m >> i) & 1;
  }
} return int c;
(2, 1):(1)
(2, 0):(0)
(0, 31):(1)

// The second mask: 1 when the callee may read an element of parameter i into a
// position that keeps it, or store into it an element that is not a list
// literal. A list whose elements the caller means to free with it cannot be
// passed there.
esc_dbit(int m, int i) {
  int c = esc_bit(m >> 15, i);
} return int c;
(32768, 0):(1)
(2, 1):(0)
(0, 15):(1)

// A function whose body cannot be read: a name that is no function of the
// program (-1), a `native`, or one a test case's `calls` clause records -- the
// recorder keeps the caller's arguments until the case judges them, so they
// must outlive the caller.
esc_opaque(int fn, string cname) {
  int r = 1;
  if(fn >= 0 && is_native(fn) == 0 && is_rec_target(cname) == 0) {
    r = 0;
  }
} return int r;
given esc_fix ((import efa_fn), "fa_sink"):(0)
given esc_fix (-1, "nosuch"):(1)
given calls_node_fixture ((import fixture_fn), "fx_target"):(1)
